<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/advanced/torts-and-liability">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Tort law allocates the costs of <strong>accidents and wrongs between private parties</strong>:
    negligence (careless harm), intentional wrongs, and strict liability (dangerous activities
    liable regardless of care). Its central engine is the negligence test — duty, breach,
    causation, damages — which structures every personal-injury and professional-liability case
    in the common-law world.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the negligence test, step by step — from the snail in the bottle to modern practice" widget="equationStep" config={tortCfg} />

  <h2>Beyond negligence</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strict liability:</strong> some activities pay for harm without fault (dangerous animals, defective products under consumer protection). Risk-creators internalise the risk.</li>
    <li><strong>Defamation:</strong> false statements damaging reputation — with free-speech defences (truth, honest opinion, public interest). The tension with expression is permanent (the journalism lesson connects).</li>
    <li><strong>Vicarious liability:</strong> employers answer for employees' torts committed in the course of work — deep pockets absorb risks businesses create.</li>
    <li><strong>Economic analysis transformed tort:</strong> Judge Learned Hand's formula (liability if prevention cost &lt; harm × probability) made tort law a risk-allocation system — influential, and contested where human dignity meets arithmetic.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Donoghue v Stevenson (1932); United States v. Carroll Towing (Hand, 1947); Lunney & Oliphant, Tort Law.</p>
</TopicPage>
